WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.260
Hi, everyone. Welcome to Models and Agents for Beginners, episode 88 for June 30, 2026.

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:11.160
Let's break down today's coolest AI news so anyone can understand it.

00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:15.960
Let's go. Here's something that's going to change how you create pictures on your phone

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:22.440
without paying a cent. Google just made Gemini's personalized image generation free for eligible

00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:28.980
users across the United States. You can now ask the chatbot to make images that match your own

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:34.880
hobbies and the stuff you already have in Google Apps like Photos or YouTube. Think of it like

00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:40.400
handing your sketchbook to an artist who has already flipped through your camera roll and knows exactly

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:46.400
which bands, games, and styles you love. The system takes your description and mixes it with that

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:51.240
background information so the results feel more personal than a standard image generator.

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:57.020
This opens the door for students working on projects, hobbyists, designing thumbnails,

00:00:57.020 --> 00:01:02.400
or anyone who wants to test wild ideas like dropping their favorite game character into their

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:08.780
own neighborhood. For teens especially, it moves AI image tools from something you hear about online

00:01:08.780 --> 00:01:15.380
to something you can open right now and experiment with on your device. The more people try these tools,

00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:19.660
the quicker everyone figures out what they do well and where they still need work.

00:01:20.540 --> 00:01:26.640
To give it a shot yourself, open the Gemini app or visit Gemini.google.com in a browser.

00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:32.700
Sign into your Google account and type a prompt such as create an image of my favorite sneakers

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:39.320
in a cyberpunk city. The output pulls from your connected data so heavier use of Google Apps

00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:45.780
leads to stronger personalization. Okay, now for my favorite part of the show, the deep dive where we

00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:51.240
look under the hood. You know how your phone keyboard watches the last few words you typed and

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:57.480
predicts what comes next based on patterns from millions of other chats. Now stretch that same idea

00:01:57.480 --> 00:02:03.760
beyond text so the system can also predict what an entire picture should contain, what a math solution

00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:10.440
likely is, or even what advice fits a tricky situation. The larger the model grows, the stronger it gets

00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:15.760
a juggling all those different jobs because it trained on one enormous collection of text that

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:22.740
includes code examples, stories, medical details, and countless other topics. That shared training is

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:29.700
why progress in one area like writing code often improves performance in another area like brainstorming

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:36.960
story ideas. Since the core ability to spot patterns carries over, some tasks remain uneven because

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:42.660
the training examples simply do not cover every subject with the same depth. In the end, the whole

00:02:42.660 --> 00:02:48.940
process is still the autocomplete trick you already know, only expanded to handle far more examples

00:02:48.940 --> 00:02:55.120
than any keyboard ever could. Once you see it that way, the results stop feeling like magic and

00:02:55.120 --> 00:03:01.360
start making sense as a scaled up version of something familiar. Let's move to cool stuff and try this

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:07.900
where I share tools you can test right away. One approach turns your phone into a quiet personal helper

00:03:07.900 --> 00:03:14.260
that lives inside your regular text messages. A user on Reddit described texting an AI agent

00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:20.020
called Folk to receive a morning summary of the calendar and weather or gentle nudges to reply

00:03:20.020 --> 00:03:26.440
to messages. The agent stays low key, works through normal language requests, and handles small

00:03:26.440 --> 00:03:32.460
reminders so you do not have to keep track of everything yourself. To try it, search your app store

00:03:32.460 --> 00:03:38.640
for Folk AI agent or similar personal agents, connect it to iMessage if the option appears,

00:03:39.320 --> 00:03:44.560
and start with a simple request such as give me a quick morning brief with today's weather and my

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:50.300
first two calendar events. See which features feel helpful and which parts still need adjustment.

00:03:51.020 --> 00:03:57.040
In Quick Bits, we cover smaller updates happening this week. The streaming service title draws a

00:03:57.040 --> 00:04:04.120
line on AI-generated music. It will stop paying artists for tracks that are 100% created by AI.

00:04:04.980 --> 00:04:11.080
The music itself stays available on the platform. Title will also automatically remove songs that

00:04:11.080 --> 00:04:17.720
try to impersonate real artists. The policy targets monetization of fully AI-created tracks.

00:04:17.720 --> 00:04:25.280
Artists receive no payments for content that meets the 100% AI criterion. Details were reported by

00:04:25.280 --> 00:04:32.800
techcrunch.com. Lawmakers proposed protecting health data shared with chatbots. A new bill would stop

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:40.200
AI companies from selling information people reveal to tools like chat GPT or Claude. Those conversations

00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:46.200
receive the same treatment as other sensitive health or location records. The proposal extends

00:04:46.200 --> 00:04:52.060
existing protections to chatbot interactions. Health and location details shared during chats

00:04:52.060 --> 00:04:59.280
form the core focus. Lawmakers aim to prevent sales of such data by AI companies. The bill was covered

00:04:59.280 --> 00:05:05.500
by the verge.com. And that's a wrap. If any of today's stories made you go, huh, that's cool,

00:05:05.860 --> 00:05:12.340
go play with it. Curiosity is how every expert started. See you tomorrow. And if you'd rather watch

